Records are sealed and unsealed by the following routine, written for this course.

```
const FIXED = Buffer.from('000102030405060708090a0b0c0d0e0f', 'hex');

function seal(plain) {
  const c = crypto.createCipheriv('aes-256-gcm', KEY, FIXED, { authTagLength: 16 });
  return Buffer.concat([c.update(plain), c.final(), c.getAuthTag()]);
}

function open(sealed) {
  const tag = sealed.subarray(sealed.length - 16);
  const body = sealed.subarray(0, sealed.length - 16);
  const d = crypto.createDecipheriv('aes-256-gcm', KEY, FIXED, { authTagLength: 16 });
  d.setAuthTag(tag);
  return Buffer.concat([d.update(body), d.final()]);
}
```

The routine is used to seal many records, and it is described as safe because the mode is authenticated. What is the flaw?

Two services need a tag that a third party cannot produce. They write it as follows, having read that a digest with a secret in front of it is unguessable.

```
function tag(secret, message) {
  return crypto.createHash('sha256')
    .update(secret).update(message)
    .digest();
}

function check(secret, message, got) {
  return tag(secret, message).equals(got);
}
```

One service uses the same secret and the same digest elsewhere to protect a fixed string it publishes, and publishes that value. What is wrong with the construction?

An endpoint decrypts a submitted record and answers differently depending on what went wrong. All of it is written for this course.

```
function handle(submitted) {
  const raw = cbcDecrypt(submitted.body, submitted.iv);
  const message = stripPadding(raw);      // returns null when the padding is wrong
  if (message === null) return reply(400, 'bad padding');
  if (!macMatches(submitted.iv, submitted.body, submitted.tag)) {
    return reply(403, 'bad tag');
  }
  return reply(200, message.toString('utf8'));
}
```

A test submits a body with one byte changed and observes the status. What has the test demonstrated?

Records are sealed with a counter-based mode, one key for the life of the deployment.

```
let counter = 0;
function seal(plain) {
  const iv = Buffer.alloc(16);
  iv.writeUInt32BE(counter++, 0);       // counter lives only in this process
  const c = crypto.createCipheriv('aes-256-ctr', KEY, iv);
  return { iv, body: Buffer.concat([c.update(plain), c.final()]) };
}

function unseal(record) {
  const c = crypto.createDecipheriv('aes-256-ctr', KEY, record.iv);
  return Buffer.concat([c.update(record.body), c.final()]);
}
```

The deployment is restarted a few times a week, and the key is reloaded from configuration on each start. What is the consequence?

A payment instruction is checked by recomputing a tag. All of it is written for this course.

```
function tagFor(instruction) {
  return hmacSha256(PAYMENT_KEY, instruction.amount + '|' + instruction.currency);
}

function accept(instruction, tag) {
  return timingSafeEqual(tagFor(instruction), tag);
}
```

An instruction arrives with its account already filled in by the caller, and the tag was produced by a legitimate service. What does the check establish?

A service stores credentials as below, having read that the point of a slow function is to stop guessing.

```
const SALT = 'a fixed string chosen once, 2021-03-04';
const WORK = 1;                      // the cost setting, as written

function store(password) {
  return deriveSlowly(password, SALT, WORK);   // derived key length 32 bytes
}
function verify(password, stored) {
  return timingSafeEqual(store(password), stored);
}
```

A database of 40,000 rows is read by someone who has the source. What does that reader get, and what is missing?

A release artefact is signed by this routine, written for this course.

```
function sign(path, privateKey) {
  const bytes = fs.readFileSync(path);
  const digest = sha256(bytes);
  return signRaw(privateKey, digest);
}

// the published record looks like this:
// { name: 'installer-v4.bin', owner: 'release-team', version: '4.2.0',
//   signature: '...' }
```

The file itself is served unchanged. A mirror serves the same file, and edits the name, the owner and the version in the published record, leaving the signature untouched. What does a verifier conclude?

A client validates what a service presents like this.

```
function accept(cert) {
  const now = Date.now();
  if (Date.parse(cert.notBefore) > now) return false;
  if (Date.parse(cert.notAfter)  < now) return false;
  if (cert.subjectAltName !== EXPECTED_NAME) return false;
  return true;
}
```

The service presents a certificate naming the expected host, inside its dates, and signed by a key that belongs to nobody the client has ever heard of. What does the client conclude?
